          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em weak bialgebra
            is a generalization of that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bialgebra"} bialgebra
            in which the comultiplication
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            is weak in the sense that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≠
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta(1)\neq 1\otimes 1
            in general; similarly the compatibility of counit with the multiplication map is weakened (counit might fail to be a morphism of algebras). (Still a special case of
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sesquialgebra"} sesquialgebra
          %p
            Correspondingly
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+Hopf+algebras"} weak Hopf algebras
            generalize
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebras"} Hopf algebras
            accordingly. Every weak Hopf algebra defines a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebroid"} Hopf algebroid
          %h3#physical_motivation Physical motivation
          %p
            This kind of structures naturally comes in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
            models relation to quantum groups a root of unity: the full symmetry algebra is not quite a quantum group at root of unity, because if it were one would have to include the nonphysical quantum dimension zero finite-dimensional quantum group representations into the (pre)Hilbert space; those are the zero norm states which do not contribute to physics (like ghosts). If one quotients by these states then the true unit of a quantum group becomes an idempotent (projector), hence one deals with weak Hopf algebras instead as a price of dealing with true, physical, Hilbert space.
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            A
            %strong weak bialgebra
            is a tuple
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi η
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,\mu,\eta,\Delta,\epsilon)
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,\mu,\eta)
            is an associative unital algebra,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,\Delta,\epsilon)
            is a coassociative counital coalgebra and the following compatibilities, (i),(ii) and (iii), hold:
          %p
            (i) the coproduct
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            is multiplicative
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta(x)\Delta(y)= \Delta(x y)
            = succeed "." do
              %strong prebialgebra
          %p
            (ii) the counit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            satisfies weak multiplicativity
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mi y
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\epsilon(x y z) = \epsilon(x y_{(1)})\epsilon(y_{(2)} z),
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mi y
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\epsilon(x y z) = \epsilon(x y_{(2)})\epsilon(y_{(1)} z).
          %p A prebialgebra satisfying the first (the second) of the above properties is said to be left (right) monoidal.
          %p (iii) Weak comultiplicativity of the unit:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Delta^{(2)} (1) = (\Delta(1) \otimes 1)(1\otimes \Delta(1))
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Delta^{(2)} (1) = (1 \otimes\Delta(1))(\Delta(1) \otimes 1)
          %p A prebialgebra satisfying the first (the second) of the above properties is said to be left (right) comonoidal.
          %p
            As usually in the context of coassociative coalgebras, we denoted
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi id
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi id
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Delta^{(2)} := (id\otimes\Delta)\Delta =
                    (\Delta\otimes id)\Delta
          %p
            A weak
            = succeed "-bialgebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a
            %strong weak Hopf algebra
            if it has a
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S:A\to A
            (which is then called an antipode) such that for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in A
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x_{(1)} S(x_{(2)}) = \epsilon(1_{(1)} x)1_{(2)},
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S(x_{(1)})x_{(2)} = 1_{(1)} \epsilon(x 1_{(2)}),
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S(x_{(1)})x_{(2)} S(x_{(3)}) = S(x)
          %p
            It follows that the antipode is antimultiplicative,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S(x y)=S(y)S(x)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Delta(S(x)) = S(x)_{(1)}\otimes S(x)_{(2)} =
                    S(x_{(2)})\otimes S(x_{(1)})
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#idempotents_projections Idempotents (“projections”)
          %p
            For every weak bialgebra there are
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Π
                    %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi Π
                    %mi R
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi^L,\Pi^R:A\to A
            defined by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Π
                    %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi Π
                    %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Pi^L(x) := \epsilon(1_{(1)} x) 1_{(2)},\,\,\,\,
                  \\Pi^R(x) := 1_{(1)}\epsilon(x 1_{(2)}).
          %p
            Expressions for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Π
                    %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi Π
                    %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi^L(x),\Pi^R(x)
            are already met above as the right hand sides in two of the axioms for the antipode. Maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Π
                    %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi Π
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi^L,\Pi^R
            are idempotents,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Π
                    %mi R
                  %msup
                    %mi Π
                    %mi R
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi Π
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi^R\Pi^R = \Pi^R
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Π
                      %mi L
                    %msup
                      %mi Π
                      %mi L
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi Π
                      %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi^L\Pi^L = \Pi^L
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi Π
                            %mi L
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi Π
                            %mi L
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                        %mtd
                          %mi ϵ
                          %mrow
                            %mo (
                            %msub
                              %mn 1
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mn 1
                                %mo ′
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi ϵ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mn 1
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mn 1
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %msub
                              %mn 1
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mn 2
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo )
                          %msub
                            %mn 1
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mn 2
                              %mo ′
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mi ϵ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mn 1
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mn 1
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi ϵ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mn 1
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mn 1
                              %mo ′
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %msub
                            %mn 1
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mn 2
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %msub
                            %mn 1
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mn 2
                              %mo ′
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                        %mtd
                          %mi ϵ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mn 1
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mn 1
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi ϵ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
